                <path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6"></path>
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="paper__abstract">
              <p>
                The implementation of security protocols often combines different languages, which
                poses a challenge to traditional verification techniques that typically assume a
                single-language environment and are insufficient to handle challenges presented by
                the interplay of different languages. We establish principles for combining multiple
                programming languages operating on different atomic types using a symbolic execution
                semantics, which facilitates the parallel composition of labelled transition systems
                and improves the analysis of complex systems by streamlining communication between
                diverse programming languages. By treating the Dolev–Yao model as a symbolic
                abstraction, our approach eliminates the need for translation between different base
                types, such as bitstrings and Dolev–Yao terms, and provides a foundation for
                securing interactions in multi-language environments.
              </p>
